Date: Sun, 21 Nov 93 14:06:36 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Investment, Philanthropy, and Oceania

Jamie Dinkelacker makes many fine comments:

(to save space, I have to elide most of them)

>    (1)  I'm flatly against philanthropy for developing a business. 

Yes, this notion of "charityware," of ideologically-driven donations
and investments is either a symptom of too much disposable income or
of a disturbing disconnection from reality. Everywhere I turn, people
are asking for donation, contributions, "spare change," and subsidies.
>From t.v. pledge breaks to the magazine "Extropy" to ocean
colonization to startups. Great motto for a startup: "Spare some
change?"

Amazingly, many free marker types think first of forming a "no-profit"
to push their ideas. Granted, this is partly an artifact of a
screwed-up tax system, but the underlying psychology is still
dramatically different when an enterprise is fundamentally a charity.

>    (7)  There's more opportunity than time, and distraction is a killer.

"Distractosis" is the disease of our generation! So many trees in the
forest that we run from tree to tree and end up lost in the forest.

I often think would-be entrepreneurs should disconnect from the Net,
cancel most of the magazines, and just dig in. The old-fashioned way.

It has yet to be proven satisforily to me that the advantages of the
Net (and other information-rich situations) outweight the incredible
time drain and "distractosis" factor. I'd like to believe it's so, but
I fear it's not.

> Side note -- recommended reading.
....
>   (6)  _The Autodesk File_ by John Walker

Very apropos for the the points I was making about Autodesk and
AutoCad in my last post. Walker also funded AMIX and Xanadu, so he
should someday have some illuminating things to say about them. (He
had a wonderful SF short story in "Microtimes" a few months back, set
in the coming decades (around 2010 as I recall). One line "And Xanadu
still hasn't been released" was a real howler.)

To Jamie's list (most of which I haven't read), I'd like to add three
others:

* Tracy Kidder, "The Soul of a New Machine," circa 1980-1. A classic
about Data General's development of its competitor to the VAX. Not
exactly about start-ups, but invaluable insights into engineering.

* C. Gordon Bell with John McNamara, "High Tech Ventures: The Guide to
Entrepreneurial Success," 1991. The single best guide I know of to
identifying and funding start-ups. I used it extensively in
researching the start-up I mentioned. And Gordon Bell was DEC's top
scientist, and has since funded many failures and a few successes

* Baird, "Engineering Your Start-Up." I haven't gotten this yet, but
my friends in the business say it's a good book.

Note that these described start-ups are all at the "traditional
start-up" level of staffing and investment, not the one-man ship Derek
has been talking about.

> Tim May posted a great insight in his "Rapture of the Future can be Cured."
> Roughly paraphrased, he pointed out that many "free market types" are
> unemployed, work in minimum wage jobs, in non-profits, or are waiting for
> the government to fund their latest idea. 

I'm still amazed at this phenomenon. Why these things are so strongly
correlated needs more study. For now, my strong advice is to run as
fast as you can from any venture that needs grants, handouts, "study"
contracts, and the like. If you need a few years to learn a new field,
to plan a product, fine. But don't confuse this with starting a
business. 

> are in the ozone. Often, these people express their idea for "business" as
> being simple and express anger and frustration at potential investors for
> not seeing the obvious, but draw a blank when asked "who will buy," "what
> will they pay," "what are the barriers to competitive market entry," or
> "what's the burdened cost of a sale." Then they get upset when investors
> look away.

Exactly! I don't consider myself to be a real businessman, just a
speculator in high tech. But I am amazed at the disdain so many of
these start-up founders have for the nuts and bolts of running a
successful business.

I guess my views were shaped in my years at Intel. I joined in 1974,
when the company was only about 5-6 years old. It was illuminating to
see Bob Noyce (co-inventor of the integrated circuit), Gordon Moore,
and Andy Grove all digging-in on whatever problems threatened to sink
the company.

IMPORTANT: Some telling things about Intel: A heavily R&D-oriented
company, and yet it began shipping products a year after formation,
thus adding revenue. Within 4 years it had unveiled the first MOS RAM,
the first EPROM, and of course the first microprocessor. It was
self-financing by its third year. Contrast this with start-ups we know
that are still doing prototype development 5 years after formation.

(And don't say, "Well, that was Intel. We're different." Intel could
just as easily sunk beneath the waves in 1972-73, as many of its
competitors did--remember Inselek and MIL? I didn't think so.---had it
dilly-dallied on getting products out quickly.

And it didn't succeed because of some "visionary" approach, some
long-range image of a microprocessor-dominated world. Everything was
cleverness in manufacturing and design and emphasis on delivery (in the early
1970s, when chip companies routinely lost their processes, Intel's
slogan was "Intel Delivers." Very well-known in its day, about the way
"Intel Inside" is known today.)

The "visions" came only because of the success. There are also obvious
evolutionary reasons: successful products will produce new
generations. The first MOS RAM paid for the development of the
microprocessor, and so on through the next dozen generations of
products.

(I've written about this extensively on this List....product cycles,
knowledge space, how the aircraft industry differs from the space
industry, the importance of intermediate products as opposed to facing
"product deserts," etc.)

Jamie makes the same point below:

> have expenses), the time for debate and lingering discussion over. Business
> success requires action, not reflection. An 80% solution that gets to
> market is always better (in business terms) than the 90% solution that's a
> year late or the 100% solution that completely misses the window of
> opportunity. It's a race, the it goes to the swift.

Exactly. Having a real product out does several things:

1. Allows learning to take place, as improvements are made, revisions
released.

2. Gives the staff something concrete to work on, to sell. Nothing is
worse than having nothing in the hands of customers.

(The product has to meet certain minimal standards, of coure. But by
the time a start-up is funded, the basic research should already be
done. This was how Sun and CISCO both got started, by commercializing
academic work at Stanford. They were able to hit the ground running.
By contrast, I know of start-ups which were still developing their
ideas several years after the funding first appeared. A disaster!)

3. Customer feedback. Learning. Mistakes get corrected. 

4. Evolutionary development. Often the real successes are not the
first products released. (Intel is the obvious example.) Having
products out in the world allows this process to unfold.

5. Revenue stream. A primary goal of all start-ups must be to achieve
self-funding (even if not very "profitable"). Otherwise, the burn rate
destroys the company or forces it to be in begging mode.

> Some entrepreneur wannabes are ignorant of market reality, and sufficiently
> arrogant that potential alliance partners, investors and customers aren't
> encouraged to take the risk of possibly dumping their cash down the tube. 

This is the main reason I haven't invested my money in nascent
start-ups until now. Too little chance of ever seeing it come back.

The localization company I mentioned in my post yesterday has avoided
most of these traps and is very product-oriented. Their business
manager is a very bright and aggressive former exec from Trimble
Navigation, the GPS localization company, and he has a solid grasp of
all of these issues. My money may vanish, but at least not because it
went down a charityware ideologically-driven rathole.

The start-up fever that is so omnipresent in the Silicon Valley is
wonderful, but folks need to wake up to the realities of starting a business.

...
> These are some opinions I've developed working around startups. I've got
> loads more, but it's now time to head to Berkeley for some social events
> and the Firesign Theater.
> 
> ah....Clem!

"Un-happy Mac-nam." 

"This is Worker, signing off."

--Tim Nym

