To tie the star trek and start-up VirtSem threads together, I offer
this editing of Jamie Dinkelacker's recent post.  In the star trek
universe, the Ferengi are a slimy race of capitalists (well, not
really, but that's how they're billed).  Anyway, they have a set
of maxims called "Rules of Acquisition" -- little aphorisms that
they can use to guide their behavior in profitable directions.

I see a definite opportunity for Extropian Rules of Acquisition;
Here I adapt Jamie's post into just that, against all custom and
usual rules of behavior about mangling other peoples' words.

Note:  I take no credit for the wisdom presented here; this is
just an editing of Jamie's post (so it shouldn't be blamed on
him either, since I've removed hedges, side points, Truth, Sense,
and attributions where appropriate in the interest of aphoristic
intensity).  This will be handy for the seminar, because from now
on we can simply refer to "Extropian Rule of Acquisition (ERA) #13"
or whatever to prove a point.

Oh, though it's probably too late, those without a sense of humor
probably shouldn't read this message.

EXTROPIAN RULES OF ACQUISITION (release 1.1)

(1)  If you need Philanthropy to grow, try again.
(2)  "Arrogant ignorance" kills startups.
(3)  Execution == Success.  Brilliant Idea == Nice Touch.
(4)  Good Engineer != Good Manager.  
(5)  Starting a business is a no-brainer.  A successful business is a
	 brain-drainer [editor's note: I don't get that last bit].
(6)  Building a sustaining organization is a team effort.  Successful
	 executives played soccer or bass guitar in their youths.
(7)  Opportunity > Time
(8)  Distraction is the mind-killer, the little death.
(9)  Ventures that are hard to fund shouldn't be funded.
(10) Smart money is.
(11) Manufacturing - Marketing = Scrap.
(12) Existing competitors will try to club a startup to death.  Be ready.
(13) The object of business is profit.
(14) Never mistake a clear vision for a short distance.
(15) Venture Capitalists rest like babies -- they sleep for two hours
     and wake up crying.
(16) It's not the guy with money's fault.
(17) Startup business is VERY hard work.
(18) Business success requires action, not reflection.
(19) An 80% solution that gets to market is always better than the 90%
	 solution that's a year late or the 100% "someday solution".
(20) Business is a race, and it goes to the swift.
(21) Know when to cut your losses.  Do it quickly.
(22) If you don't know where the market is today, you sure as hell don't
	 know where it'll be tomorrow.
(23) Oral contraceptives and BIC pens are the exceptions, not the rule.
(24) The world ain't gonna beat a path to your door.
(25) If you think PR is evil, you're in the wrong game.
(26) If you think advertising is evil, you're in the wrong game.
(27) Get qualified people to do the evil things.
(28) Market size is a boundary value on success.  You are not alone in
	 knowing that value.
(29) Be explicit about your assumptions.
(30) R&D costs money, lotsa money.
(31) Clerical and support staff cost money, lotsa money.
(32) All those who see themselves at the top of the food chain are
	 burdensome overhead.
(33) Salespeople pay everybody's salaries. Be nice to them; pay them well.
(34) Benefits sell and features don't.
(35) Packaging and positioning are crucial.
(36) Identify a niche.
(37) Relentlessly pursue your target market.
(38) Streamline internal operations as much as possible.
(39) Be good to workers. Give them good lighting, ergonomic furniture,
	 and the best tools. 
(40) Do not tolerate anybody who isn't a team player. Flush them immediately.
(41) Do not tolerate anybody who is tardy. Flush them immediately.
(42) Morale counts much more in a startup than in larger firms.
(43) Learn how to hire good seeds.
(44) Business means an office system.
(45) Profitable business is a two fold execution play: inside and out.
(46) Look for alliances and "big wins" that are mutually beneficial.
(47) There's always more work than can get done.
(48) Perfectionists starve.
(49) Speed is life.
(50) Set priorties.  Stick to them.
(51) Have an exit strategy.
(52) Time and money spent posting to Extropians goes in your competitor's
