From: andreag@csmil.umich.edu
Subject: surfing for pay

I like this idea, so I'm going to throw as much cold 
water on it as I can.  I have some tidbits of information
(which I will give away free) and some opinions as someone
fascinated by the "information retrieval" industry.

I recall a company mentioned by the Economist (perhaps in the
story they did on AMiX) that sounds a lot like David Murray's
AnswerMan.  But this company doesn't focus exclusively on using 
Net or online sources.  They have a network of stringers, such
as professors or other experts in a field, whom they pay to
answer questions about their area of expertise.  Subscribers
pay a sizable retainer and fees for each topic researched,
plus a premium for speed.  (I could look up the date the 
article appeared in the Economist, but I can't access any
online abstracting and indexing services from here, and the
local CD-ROM system isn't that up-to-date :)  So, don't think
that you will be entering a brand new field.

The other insight I have is that you probably haven't solved
the most vexing problem of information retrieval, which I will
losely term "indexing".  Your system may start out easy to use,
when the client can send someone in the front office a message
about what their problem is and the front office goes "oh yes.
That's Tim May's area.  I'll forward your query to him and he
will be in touch."  But systems like that don't scale up.  They
work well when there is a simple algorithm to identify where to 
look for the info, or when the infobase is small enough for one
or two human minds to have a good intuitive feel for where things
should be.  Once you have a whole bunch of people participating,
and the questions get less than clear, it will get hard to figure
out who your clients should talk to.  

At this point, your mind should be roiling with cool high-tech
ways to match clients with consultants.  Good.  But talk to
people in the field before you sink money into it.  Some wheels
have been invented and broken already, others have been invented
but never really tested.  AnswerMan (see p.s.) has a leg up because
the interesting thing is the type of information it will sell,
and it may never get big enough to hit this wall.  But remember
that it's there.

If I think of anything relevant, I'll try to compose a more
coherent post.

Good Luck,

Andrea Gallagher


