Date: Sun, 21 Nov 93 16:12:17 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Stealing Ideas and Commercializing Them

I want to follow-up on my point about how Sun Microsystems and CISCO
Systems (or Cisco, I never can remember the origin of the name) both
got started by commercializing the work already done at Stanford.

(At least one book has been written about Sun, which I haven't read,
but I knew a lot of folks at Sun, and still do. The founders of Cisco
were "friends of a friend"--in Bay Area SF fandom--and they each walked
away with more than one hundred million dollars in cash.)

If you want to fund a company, which would you prefer:

Choice 1: Start with a long-range vision. Get funding. Hire people to
begin researching and developing your vision. Spend several years
laying the groundwork.

or,

Choice 2: Take the knowledge of how to do something gained at your
former employer, or your grad school, and quickly get a product to
market using this knowledge. Once a revenue stream is established,
develop more products.

I'd pick Choice 2. Sun Microsystems built on work already done at
Stanford ("SUN" = "Stanford University Network"), as did Cisco. In
both cases, Stanford either understimated the potential market (which
can be forgiven, for many reasons) or had no interest in getting into
the business themselves (for lots of reasons). They licensed their
rights to these companies and the rest is history.

And this pattern is very common in successful companies. Intel, for
example, in essence "stole" the silicon-gate MOS process the founders
had developed while at Fairchild: the seminal paper on silicon gate
was written by Fairchild researchers Les Vadasz, Andy Grove, Tom Rowe,
and Gordon Moore. Within a year, all of them were at Intel. Guess what
their first technology and product was?

(In today's climate, surely grounds for an injuntion barring them from
using what they'd learned. Back then, things were looser.)

I could name several other conspicuous examples. Apple's
commericialization of the Xerox PARC work on user interfaces and
workstations, which Xerox was foolish enough to freely demonstrate to
Steve Jobs and others, Seymour Cray's experience building
supercomputers for CDC in the 1960s, and nearly all chip companies, in
which the founders took at least the basic ideas and knowhow they'd
developed at their previous jobs to allow them to hit the ground
running.

Money runs out really fast once that "main stage" has been ignited. If
you're not shipping a product within 2 years of funding, look out!

And probably the only way to meet this aggressive rollout shce

By contrast--and perhaps this is the real key--neither Xanadu nor AMIX
were built on already-running programs or available technology. Never
before had a true hypertext system been developed, at least not one
like Nelson envision...the Brown University system and similar
experiments at Xerox differed in important ways, and in any case were
considered to be flawed by the Xanadu folks and hence were not used as
the foundation of their efforts.

AMIX was less far-out, and various other electronically-mediated
markets had of course already existed for years. But AMIX was
definitely not commercializing something that had already been built.

(AMIX, to its credit, got a running version by late 1991, and may
have failed for other reasons. Xanadu, however, began 20-30 years ago
as a vision of Ted Nelson's--I read his vision in "Computer Lib" in
1976--and never became operational enough for a real demo....I hope I
am not mischaracterizing this, but I asked when I could get a demo of
Xanadu and one never seemed to be available. Makes it hard to develop
tools and use evolutionary development when no working model exists.
The folks involved in Memex have claimed that a working demo now
exists, sort of, but funding seems to be competely dried up now.
Another story.)

The moral: Steal when you can!

The less harsh version of this moral: Work on what you know can be
done. If basic research and development is needed on an idea, don't
try to build a start-up around it. Let a university or established R&D
facility pay for it, then either license the technology or find a way
to exploit it without getting sued.

Thus, by this analysis, Derek's best shot at success probably involves
commercializing something related to neural nets that he worked on in
grad school, as opposed to casting about for something completely new.
(If he's happy with writing a small one-person software app, this may
not be so important a factor).

I think this line of reasoning--that commercialization should arise
once an idea has already been realized--is important. I'd be
interested in your reactions.

--Tim May

