From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: PHYSICS: Theory and experiment

Derek Zahn asks, why bother with the SSC and other giga-expensive
accelerators to measure the masses of a couple of exotic particles?  A
theory of everything should give specific predictions for all particle
masses, including many already known, so how does the mass of a Higgs
particle (which we hope the SSC will yield) help?  Excellent question. 
I went back and consulted _Dreams of a Final Theory_ by Steve Weinberg. 
Weinberg is a strong supporter of the SSC and also of string theory (a
possible TOE) so it should be interesting to see how he resolves the
dilemma.

As far as I can make out Weinberg supports the SSC as a safety net
option in case string theory goes nowhere.  On page 174 he says "We
simply have to hope either that string theory will become more
successful [ie make predictions] or that new experiments [the SSC] will
open up progress in other directions".  I can't help wondering how many
theorists could be employed for the cost of the SSC and other
accelerators.

If I had a choice of where to spend billions on hardware for fundamental
physics research I'd choose telescopes (like Hubble, but not botched!)
over accelerators since they are likely to tell us more, given the
interplay between particle physics and cosmology.  Telescopes also have
other very important benefits like the pretty colour blow-ups of M31 and
NGC 2997 that adorn my bedroom walls.  (Also, I guess, they'll be useful
for space exploration.)

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

