> 
> If I had a choice of where to spend billions on hardware for fundamental
> physics research I'd choose telescopes (like Hubble, but not botched!)
> over accelerators since they are likely to tell us more, given the
> interplay between particle physics and cosmology.  Telescopes also have
> other very important benefits like the pretty colour blow-ups of M31 and
> NGC 2997 that adorn my bedroom walls.  (Also, I guess, they'll be useful
> for space exploration.)

For some reason, I had been thinking Mike was a supporter of the
"Dreams of a Final Theory" and "The God Particle" visions. I guess
not.

There's no longer a single "Big Science" project I can support. The
mostly-privately-funded Keck Telescope (some of Univ. of Calif. and
Univ. of Hawaii funding, for operations) is my ideal of how this kind
of research ought to be done.

And like Mike, if the government feels it has to fund research, let it
fund mathematics, computer science theory, linguistics, information
theory, and theoretical physicists (_not_ the high-energy folks
necessarily). 

Spending $15 billion to get a measurement of the Higgs boson mass is
simply not a good return on investment. It would be--perhaps--in a
world of unlimited wealth (but then $10 billion wouldn't be $10
billion, it would be 25 cents, for example). But not when basic theory
is drying up around the country, when simple experiments can't get
funding, and when the tax burden is already killing jobs.

A familiar story, so I'll stop.

--Tim May


