[warning: this is another one of those personal experience posts.
Amara, I remember writing a disclaimer like this last year :-) 
Submitted in case it has any value to list members who are considering
similar explorations]

> 	We had one bad trip out of hundreds -- one guy went into a depression
> 	from some guilt feelings about not living up to his mother's
> 	expectations. He didn't hurt anyone, but he made a pain in the ass of
> 	himself for about 6 hours.
> 
> Hm.  I can easily see myself doing something like this -- I have
> plenty to deal with.  I take that as another reason to go with a
> professional -- they get paid to deal with my uncontrolled angst.

I had a similar experience once. The trip ended in 12 hours but it
took me about nine MONTHS to regain perspective on the insights. THe
problem is that under the influence of LSD you are VERY vulnerable. A
random insight can "burn in" to your mental EPROM and take a long time
to deal with. The revalations I had were, I believe to this day ,
ACCURATE, and I'm not sure when or if I would have realized them
without the drug. However, without a trusted guide, I didn't have any
PERSPECTIVE on the insights.
  On the other hand, at the height of an LSD trip I'm not sure I could
have verbalized the thought clearly enough for a guide to have told me
something like "are there two sides to that?" Under LSD, I have tended
to see everything as a multidimensional web of interconnected
knowledge. This is generally true, but under LSD I tend to see the
whole thing at ONCE and it's terribly hard to pick which small subset
should be put into words. I call this "lack of perspective" for want
of a better word. To this day, (gosh, I think today might be the 15th
anniversary of that particular trip!) the visions I saw on that trip
are still vivid, and I still use what I learned. The very first time I
took acid, I discovered a view of my Self that still shapes me. I took
acid less than a dozen times and was not able to repeat revalations; I
formed a personal belief that I had exhausted what I had to learn from
the experience and would not do it again. I still feel strongly that
it's a gamble. Now that I'm in my 30's, I have solved certain problems
in my life, and don't feel like taking the gambles that I did in my
20's. Perhaps more drug-based exploration would open up a new period
of growth; perhaps it would destroy some of what I've carefully built.
   At this point in my life, the gambles I am taking are in building a
career, choosing someone to start a family with, other less
metaphysical issues...perhaps after I'm done raising kids I will go
back out onto the cosmic highway....



  

