Date: Sun, 21 Nov 1993 23:34:31 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Exit strategies

For a startup that seeks investor funding from the outset, it's critical to
have several "exit strategies" viz. how the investors are going to get
their money out of the deal.

Overall, note that investors typically get "a piece" of the company. This
means some amount of equity (often common [voting] stock, sometimess
preferred, othertimes warrants on common or preferred). Investors often
want a seat (or two, or three) on the board of directors as well. It's
their money and they're going to do what they can to protect it. 

"Valuation" is another key aspect of investing in startups. Roughly
speaking, valuation indicates how much the company is worth today and over
the next several years, typically 1-3. Investors come up with their own
formulae for how much a company is worth (results of "due dilligence"), how
much money they'll put in the deal, and how big a chunk of the company they
want to do this. 

(The rubber meets the road with what's called the "Term Sheet" where the
investor says they want your first and last born, your mate, gold teeth,
prized guitar, and 80% of the company. I'm being facetious, but not much.)

Investors invest with an eye to return on investment (ROI), not
philanthropy. So, how do they get their money out of the deal, and how to
they realize their return?

Several straightforward strategies are used. (Note, the examples given here
are rough approximations and the purists will object slightly to the
liberties I've taken with the language and scenarios, but I prefer not to
write a ten thousand word missive to get these points across...the examples
given are much more right than they're wrong :-)

(1) BUY OUT. Startup is purchased by another company as either a 
        strategic fit or to kill competition. Investors "cash out" 
        by selling their shares to the purchasing company. Management
        (or a good portion of it) often walks away and goes on to 
        another project.

(2) IPO. Initial public offering. Company executives repeatedly kiss 
        the ass of the SEC and certain investment bankers and the 
        stock is offered for sale on an exchange (e.g., NASD). 
        Institutions and the public buy up stock and the investors
        sell off part of theirs during this (hopefully) feeding 
        frenzy. Management typically stays in place and the company
        has mucho cash on hand for expansion from having sold treasury
        shares.

(3) REMAIN PRIVATE. If the company is doing a whopping business with
        VERY good net margins, some investors may wish to hang on their stock
        and get paid by a dividend stream. Sometimes this means converting
        warrants to a balance of preferred (paid first) and common (voting)
        stock. This one is better for the management, but least good for the
        investors, typically, because it keeps the principal of the 
        investment non-liquid (not cash or quickly convertible assets) for
        much longer, inhibiting them from putting the money in another 
        venture to give a good return.

=========

Equally important exit strategies detail what to do if it all goes bad.
These involve:
        (a) can parts of the startup be sold off to cut losses and
            keep the wolves away allowing the owners to walk away
            unscathed;
        (b) which creditors get paid in which order;
        (c) can bankruptcy be avoided (Chapter 7 = death, 
            Chapter 11 = reorganization, roughtly a 10% chance of life).
        (d) which assets (including intellectual property) get sold
            in which order (its good to have likely buyers in mind).
        (e) can joint ventures or strategic alliances be established
            that allows a graceful transition out of business, a chance
            for rebirth, or a restart with better execution and lower
            burn (i.e., recurring expenses).

=========== 

One last point to make in this post. Depending on the exit strategy
targeted from those listed above, the startup's management should focus on
that strategy from day one. In particular, if an IPO is the goal, it's wise
to begin tailoring all the startup metrics from day one to present an
appealing profile to "the street" (i.e., "wall street" analysts). This has
implications for lease/buy decisions, make/buy decisions, staffing ... ...
... Having a Harvard or Wharton MBA (finance) on your executive team can
only help. If the goal is to stay private (probably not make as much money
for the investors) other decisions may well be made (build asset base) or
staff up instead of depending on contractors and suppliers (whose schedules
can make a startup plead for mercy).

Basic rule: investors seek favorable returns, not good feelings. In other
words, cash makes them feel the best.

Just some more thoughts.....

